soc: sunxi: cpu-comm: never write into a full TX FIFO

What our send path does today: it writes the word to MSG_DATA at 0x03003874
without ever looking at the FIFO count at 0x03003864, then sets BIT(3) in
TX_IRQ_EN at 0x03003830, waits 10 us and clears it again. Two things about
that are worth separating, and only one of them is settled.

Settled (AP3s section 2, from the vendor's own ISR sunxi_msgbox_irq
@0xC07DACC0): the block's TX FIFO is eight words deep, the vendor pushes a
word only while the count register differs from 8, and a word written into a
full FIFO is lost with no error and no trace. We never looked. This patch
adds the look: the count is read before the write, and at 8 or above the
word is not written, one ratelimited line names the count and the message
type, and the caller gets -EBUSY. SendCommLow() already returns int and now
returns that; the "msgbox not mapped" path returns -ENODEV for the same
reason. Nothing else about the wire changes.

Does it ever get full at our message rate? On a healthy link, no. A round
trip costs the ARM two words (the CALL and the RETURN_ACK, or the RETURN and
the CALL_ACK), the MIPS pops each one as it dispatches, and the count after
a write reads 0 or 1. The counter only climbs when the far side is not
draining at all - which is exactly doku/66's picture, 13 and 14 on a MIPS
that doku/67 later found parked by `h713_disp auto 0x30 logo`, and with the
doorbell block still doubled (0014d) those were two words per message. So
the guard costs a healthy HY310 one extra register read per doorbell and
fires on nothing; where it fires, it turns a silent loss into a line in the
log. The count of 8 is the vendor's full mark and the only documented one;
if a device run ever shows a healthy link standing above 8, that is the
number to raise, and it is one define.

NOT settled, and therefore not touched: the TX_IRQ_EN pulse. In the vendor
driver the bit is a level, "I have bytes to push", set by
sunxi_rpmsg_edp_send @0xC07DABEC and cleared by the ISR once the buffer is
drained; the receiver's doorbell is the FIFO write itself and nothing is
ever pulsed. Our pulse arms a TX interrupt for ten microseconds on a bank
whose GIC line 0014c deliberately does not request, and it can leave the TX
status bit at 0x03003834 latched, because nobody writes it back. But AP3s
does not pick an end state: it offers "drop the pulse" or "keep it and add
the W1C of 0x03003834", and the evidence for the edge claim in our own
comment is confounded, so choosing between them from the report alone is not
possible. The comment in the code now says so; the decision needs the device
run below. Setting the bit as a level without a TX ISR to clear it would be
the one clearly wrong option.

What the HY310 sees: nothing, unless the MIPS has stopped draining. The word
on the wire, the pulse and the register addresses are unchanged.

FOLLOW-UP, device (HY310), settles AP3s F2:
  1. Boot with the MIPS running, then
       cat /proc/interrupts | grep -E '46|108|109'
     and note the counts.
  2. Drive traffic (h713-tv or the cpu_comm debugfs "call" file), repeat 1.
     Expected: SPI 46 climbs, SPI 108 does not appear at all (its line is
     not requested), no "fifo full" line in dmesg.
  3. devmem2 0x03003834 (read only). Expected if the pulse latches a status
     nobody clears: bit 3 set. That read decides between "drop the pulse"
     and "W1C 0x03003834 after it".
  4. devmem2 0x03003864 (read only) directly after nine sends with the MIPS
     parked. Expected 8 if the vendor's full mark is this block's depth;
     a higher number means the counter runs past the depth and AP3s section
     12's first open question is answered the other way.

Source: umbau/work/AP3s/REPORT.txt sections 2, 11 and 12 and
umbau/re-apps/AP3s/REVIEW.md (F2). Follow-up to 0014a; 0014a to 0014e stay
byte-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm.h
@@ -758,7 +758,7 @@ unsigned long  getShareSeqR(u32 cpu, u32 dir);
 unsigned long  getShareSeqW(u32 cpu, u32 dir);
 
 /* export for other modules (tvtop, decd, etc.) */
-void sunxi_cpu_comm_send_intr_to_mips(u32 msg_type, u32 type, u32 value);
+int sunxi_cpu_comm_send_intr_to_mips(u32 msg_type, u32 type, u32 value);
 
 /* H713 direct msgbox IRQ handler (replaces mailbox framework) */
 int  cpu_comm_msgbox_request_irq(int irq, void *callback);
diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_hw.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_hw.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_hw.c
@@ -90,6 +90,16 @@ static void __iomem *msgbox_ctrl_base;	/* ioremap of 0x03003000 */
 #define H713_MSGBOX_VERSION_ALIVE 0x00020000
 #define H713_MSGBOX_TX_IRQ_BIT	BIT(3)	/* BIT(2*port + 1), MIPS = port 1 */
 
+/*
+ * The vendor's own msgbox ISR (sunxi_msgbox_irq @0xC07DACC0, AP3s section 2)
+ * pushes a word only while the FIFO count register differs from 8, so 8 is
+ * the full mark of this block and a word written past it is lost without a
+ * trace. Our send path never looked. On a healthy link the MIPS drains the
+ * FIFO and this count stays at 0 or 1; it only climbs when the far side is
+ * not draining at all, which is exactly the state worth reporting.
+ */
+#define H713_MSGBOX_TX_FIFO_DEPTH	8
+
 /*
  * Every word in the RX FIFO is sent by the display MIPS, so the source
  * is not encoded in the message and does not need decoding. This is
@@ -1245,14 +1255,18 @@ void comm_SpinLockCleanUpInCPUReset(u32 cpu_id)
  * @msg_type: 0=CALL, 1=RETURN, 2=CALL_ACK, 3=RETURN_ACK
  * @type:     stock intr_type; not part of the wire format, ignored
  * @value:    payload value (unused in current path)
+ *
+ * Returns 0 when the word was written, -ENODEV when the msgbox is not
+ * mapped, and -EBUSY when the TX FIFO is full - in that last case the word
+ * would have been lost without a trace, so it is not written at all.
  */
-void sunxi_cpu_comm_send_intr_to_mips(u32 msg_type, u32 type, u32 value)
+int sunxi_cpu_comm_send_intr_to_mips(u32 msg_type, u32 type, u32 value)
 {
-	u32 raw;
+	u32 raw, fifo;
 
 	if (!msgbox_ctrl_base) {
 		pr_err("cpu_comm: msgbox not mapped, can't send intr!\n");
-		return;
+		return -ENODEV;
 	}
 
 	/*
@@ -1278,9 +1292,27 @@ void sunxi_cpu_comm_send_intr_to_mips(u32 msg_type, u32 type, u32 value)
 	 * Dazu der TX_IRQ_EN-Puls: die H713-Msgbox ist flankengetriggert,
 	 * nicht pegelgetriggert wie die des H6. MSG_DATA allein lässt die
 	 * Nachricht in der FIFO liegen und weckt den Empfänger nicht.
+	 *
+	 * The paragraph above is not settled and the pulse is left alone here
+	 * on purpose. In the vendor driver TX_IRQ_EN is a level meaning "I
+	 * have bytes to push", set by sunxi_rpmsg_edp_send @0xC07DABEC and
+	 * cleared by the ISR when the buffer is drained; the receiver's
+	 * doorbell is the FIFO write itself, and nothing there is ever pulsed
+	 * (AP3s section 2). The measurement behind the edge claim is
+	 * confounded - doku/66 saw the FIFO count rise with the pulse in
+	 * place, and doku/67 found the real cause, a MIPS parked by
+	 * `h713_disp auto 0x30 logo`. Deciding this needs one device run:
+	 * see the FOLLOW-UP in 0014f.
 	 */
 	raw = type & 0x3;
 
+	fifo = readl(msgbox_ctrl_base + H713_MSGBOX_TX_FIFO);
+	if (fifo >= H713_MSGBOX_TX_FIFO_DEPTH) {
+		pr_warn_ratelimited("cpu_comm: msgbox tx fifo full (%u), %s not sent\n",
+				    fifo, cpu_comm_msgbox_type_name(raw));
+		return -EBUSY;
+	}
+
 	writel(raw, msgbox_ctrl_base + H713_MSGBOX_TX_DATA);
 	writel(H713_MSGBOX_TX_IRQ_BIT, msgbox_ctrl_base + H713_MSGBOX_TX_IRQ_EN);
 	udelay(10);
@@ -1289,6 +1321,7 @@ void sunxi_cpu_comm_send_intr_to_mips(u32 msg_type, u32 type, u32 value)
 	pr_debug("cpu_comm: msgbox tx raw=0x%08x (%s), fifo now %u\n",
 			    raw, cpu_comm_msgbox_type_name(raw),
 			    readl(msgbox_ctrl_base + H713_MSGBOX_TX_FIFO));
+	return 0;
 }
 
 /*
diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c
@@ -155,9 +155,9 @@ int SendCommLow(u8 *seq, u32 slot, u8 type, u32 param, u32 sem_ref)
 	{
 		u32 comm_type = (dir == 0) ? MSG_TYPE_CALL : MSG_TYPE_RETURN;
 
-		sunxi_cpu_comm_send_intr_to_mips(0 /*sender=ARM*/, comm_type, 0);
+		return sunxi_cpu_comm_send_intr_to_mips(0 /*sender=ARM*/,
+							comm_type, 0);
 	}
-	return 0;
 }
 
 /* ── SendComm2CPUEx — Extended message send ────────────────── */
